        "id": 48346,
        "name": "Tenchi wo Kurau II: Shokatsu Koumei-den",
        "summary": "A Japanese roleplaying game published for the NES in 1991. Based on the manga Tenchi wo Kurau.\n\nTenchi o Kurau II: Shokatsu Koumei-den (\"The Devouring of Heaven and Earth II: The Story of King Ming\") is a turn based roleplaying and strategy game developed and published for the Nintendo Entertainment System by Capcom on April 5th, 1991 in Japan. It is the second NES RPG produced by Capcom based upon the Tenchi wo Kurau manga series, the first being Destiny of an Emperor released in 1989.\n\nGameplay:\nA traditional Japanese roleplaying game at its core, Tenchi o Kurai II puts the player in control of a party of warriors and mages as they try to take down a great foe that threatens the peace of their land. Throughout there are random encounters, boss battles, an experience system and valuable and/or useful loot.\n\nThe player's party can hold up to seven members at a time, with one members acting as a \"tactician\". The tactician determines the list of tactics available for use by the player during battle.",
        "cover_url": "https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co539w.jpg",
        "release_date": 670809600,
        "rating": null,
        "rating_count": null,
        "genres": "Role-playing (RPG),Strategy",
        "platform_ids": "99",
        "external_url": "https://www.igdb.com/games/tenchi-wo-kurau-ii-shokatsu-koumei-den",
        "cached_at": 1778663176
      },
      {
        "id": 48348,
        "name": "Parallel World",
        "summary": "Parallel World is a puzzle video game developed by EIM and published by Varie. It was released in Japan for the Family Computer on August 10, 1990.\n\nThe player and his girlfriend must find their way back home after being sucked into an alternate universe.\n\nA magnificent castle full of 25 different game worlds block their progress, however, and they are filled with enemies on every stage. The player has an overhead view to destroy the enemies for extra loot. Given a strict time limit of 100 seconds to solve each puzzle, the game rewards fast puzzle solvers. The first player controls the male while the second player controls the female. Players only start with three lives and lose them when time runs out or when a monster comes into contact with him/her.\n\nEnemies in the game include springs, rollers, witches, and zombies.",
        "cover_url": "https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2u09.jpg",
        "release_date": 652924800,
        "rating": null,
